11.1  Hashes, encryption and signatures

TL;DR
  - A hash turns any data into a short fingerprint (SHA-256: 32 bytes). Same
    input, same hash; you can't go back, nor find two inputs with the same hash.
  - Encryption needs a key to read the data again. With symmetric encryption
    (AES, ChaCha20) one shared key both locks and unlocks.
  - A signature proves who wrote something and that it wasn't changed: made with
    a private key, checked with the public one (11.2).

      sha256sum ubuntu.iso            # compare with the published hash
      sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing

On your machine
  Trusted   3 certificate authorities, in /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
  AES       aes-aesni, with the CPU's AES instructions · SHA-256: sha256-ni
  RNG       hardware generator tpm-rng-0 feeds the kernel
  TPM       version 2: can hold keys and seal disk keys

Pitfalls
  - MD5 and SHA-1 are broken against attackers: fine to spot accidental
    corruption, never for security.
  - Passwords are stored with slow hashes made for it (yescrypt, bcrypt,
    Argon2), never a plain SHA-256: fast hashes make guessing fast.
  - Encryption without authentication lets an attacker alter data unnoticed: use
    modes like AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305.
  - Don't invent your own scheme: use a library's high-level API (libsodium,
    your language's standard crypto).

Deep dive

  ▾ Why is a hash enough to check a download?

    If the hash on the official site matches the file's, the file is the one
    they published: finding a different file with the same SHA-256 is out of
    reach. The hash itself must come from a trusted place (an HTTPS page or a
    signed file), otherwise an attacker swaps both.

  ▾ How fast is encryption today?

    With the CPU's AES instructions (AES-NI on x86, the crypto extensions on
    ARM), one core encrypts several gigabytes per second: faster than most disks
    and networks. That's why encrypting disks and all web traffic costs almost
    nothing now. Chips without them use ChaCha20, fast in plain software.

Related
  Commands  sha256sum, openssl
  Paths     /proc/crypto
  See also  idk 11.2 Public-key cryptography
  See also  idk 11.4 Random numbers: /dev/urandom and entropy
